00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:10.090
KRISTIE MANN: Hello, everyone,
and welcome to our talk

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:12.510
on persistent memory.

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:14.770
Now I'm going to ask
a question, and I

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:16.840
want to see a lot
of raised hands,

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:19.300
or you're going to
hurt my feelings.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:20.860
I love this guy.

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:21.850
All right.

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:25.060
So how many of you remember
a world before the cloud

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:27.790
and before data?

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:29.080
Thank you.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:30.100
All right.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.229
So our world is changing.

00:00:32.229 --> 00:00:35.020
And I'm going to tell you
a personal experience going

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:39.640
to the Grand Canyon with my
brother, father, mother--

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:42.370
summer vacation when
I was growing up.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:44.110
And we went to go
to the Grand Canyon.

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:46.600
And dad says, hey, kids,
let's get in the station wagon

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:48.400
with the wood paneled sides.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:50.980
And let's go get a triptych.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:54.250
How many of you know
what a triptych is?

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:55.050
Uh oh.

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:56.470
Now I'm really old.

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:58.625
OK, so you would go to AAA.

00:00:58.625 --> 00:01:01.000
They would print out the route
that you were going to go.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.375
And they would help you map
along the way what you wanted

00:01:03.375 --> 00:01:04.840
to stop at, what
was along the way,

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:08.500
and you'd get this book of
maps for the whole trip.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.370
We thought that was so cool.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.100
And that's what we were doing.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.822
In that world, we
were not generating

00:01:14.822 --> 00:01:16.780
mountains, and mountains,
and mountains of data

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:18.560
like we are today.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:20.302
Fast forward to MapQuest.

00:01:20.302 --> 00:01:22.510
I thought that was the best
thing since sliced bread,

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:23.010
right?

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:26.140
I could print out on demand
where I wanted to go.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:28.480
So I was starting
to consume data,

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:31.090
but I was just at the
tip of the iceberg.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.640
And then we had turn
by turn directions.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:36.290
For those of you that are
directionally challenged,

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:37.400
that was life changing.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.760
I don't go anywhere
without my phone.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:41.830
Add to that now the
world of ways, where

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:43.240
we're crowdsourcing the data.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:45.460
We're actually generating
data as we drive

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:46.948
for the rest of the world.

00:01:46.948 --> 00:01:48.865
You guys know where I'm
going with this story.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:55.580
So let's look at
the amount of data

00:01:55.580 --> 00:02:00.590
that's being produced as we
speak, in today's day and age.

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:02.360
I quote these
numbers over and over

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:04.820
again because they give them
to me in a slide to quote.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:07.560
But let's think about what
we're doing with that data.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.759
There's zettabytes of data
that businesses are producing,

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:12.800
but we're not able to
take advantage of it

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.110
because it's just
sitting in repositories,

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:17.900
and it's a liability
rather than an asset.

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:19.520
We believe we have
a technology that

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:22.190
can help us on the
journey to changing that

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:27.320
so that when we reach
175 zettabytes in 2025,

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:30.230
the world is able to process
every inch of that data,

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:32.990
make smarter decisions,
and do more with that data.

00:02:35.818 --> 00:02:38.110
So I'm going to talk to you
about the most loved memory

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:39.010
storage hierarchy.

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:41.290
We've all seen it
a million times.

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:43.780
I've had it drawn
this way in particular

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.300
because we tend to think
of it as a continuum.

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:47.960
But it's not really a continuum.

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:51.310
There's big gaps in
performance and in cost

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:54.160
as you jump between
DRAM and storage

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:56.380
and which is the fast
storage, then down to tape

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:57.760
and hard drive.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.760
And for 50 years, the
architects of the world

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:03.310
have had to adjust the
way that they programmed

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:05.320
to minimize their memory usage.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:09.230
Because it's very expensive,
and the capacity is limited.

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:12.310
We also see that DRAM isn't
scaling at the same rate

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:13.940
that our data is.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:17.380
And so what if we could have
this amazing technology where

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:21.550
we could have double,
triple the capacity,

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:24.010
give up very little
of the performance,

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:25.660
and do it in an affordable way?

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:29.030
That seems like a
dream come true.

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:33.260
And today, I'm here to announce
we just went live last week.

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:35.570
This is a half a
terabyte in my hand.

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:39.230
Whoever thought we
would see that day?

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:42.230
And this is now broadly
available with Intel's Xeon

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:44.303
Cascade Lake CPUs.

00:03:44.303 --> 00:03:46.220
And we believe that we're
going to be changing

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:48.110
the world with this technology.

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:50.450
We're just beginning
on the journey.

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:52.410
So I'll tell you a
little bit about it.

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:57.500
It comes in modules 128,
256, and 512 gigabyte.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:00.943
To help level set 32
gigabytes to maybe 64

00:04:00.943 --> 00:04:02.360
is kind of a sweet
spot for today,

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:05.400
so it's much higher capacity.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:08.950
It also has the ability to
be spoken to in either memory

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:10.660
language or storage language.

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:16.110
So it's byte addressable and
bit addressable in 4k blocks.

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:17.550
It has multiple modes.

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:19.745
We have a memory mode
and an app direct mode.

00:04:19.745 --> 00:04:21.120
And I'll talk to
you a little bit

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.287
about that because I know
several of you in the room

00:04:23.287 --> 00:04:26.580
are developers, and you want to
know what this means for you.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:27.960
And it's natively persistent.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:31.260
So the data is at
rest in the CPU block.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:33.820
That changes the way we
think about our data.

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:35.490
It's also highly secure.

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:37.470
We have hardware encryption.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:41.010
It fits in DDR4 DIMM slots,
and it's also physically

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:42.700
the same form factor.

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:45.450
So it's not terribly intrusive
to the system designs

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:46.320
that we have today.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:51.940
So let me touch a
little bit on the modes.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:54.130
We have an app direct mode.

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:57.430
And that just basically
means that the application

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:00.910
is able to smartly place
the data where it belongs.

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:03.700
If it's hot data that's being
used on a regular basis,

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:06.010
it can reside in the DRAM.

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:08.920
If it's colder data
or cooler data,

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:13.300
it can reside in
either the Apache--

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:15.760
I just started to call
it by its code name--

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:18.610
in the Intel Optane
DC persistent memory

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:20.260
or in storage.

00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:23.800
And we have a whole
range of ISV partners

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:26.560
that are helping to
redefine and redevelop

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:28.120
the architectures
of their software

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:31.090
to take advantage of
this new capability.

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:33.100
The application
and the OS actually

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:36.290
see two separate
distinct pools of memory.

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:40.240
And that's what is
encompassed in the app direct.

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:43.120
When you look at memory
mode, we have this mode

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:44.950
for applications
that haven't yet

00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:48.910
been optimized to take
advantage of the capability.

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:51.580
And so what we like
to say is that it's

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:54.280
fairly easy to get in
and try the technology.

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:58.330
The application and the OS
sees a single pool of memory

00:05:58.330 --> 00:05:59.960
like it always does.

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:02.110
And it doesn't realize
that the DRAM that's

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:05.380
installed with the Optane
DC persistent memory

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:09.080
is actually being used as a
cache to help speed things up.

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:11.260
So it's fairly transparent.

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:14.230
You don't always get the maximum
benefit of the technology

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:15.610
when you're in memory mode.

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:17.530
To get that best
performance you want

00:06:17.530 --> 00:06:20.680
to be app direct optimized.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:22.930
But this allows you to get
in and try the technology

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:26.770
and still take advantage of the
capacity and the affordability

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:27.790
that the product brings.

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:35.060
So when you look at the new
way that we envision the memory

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:39.170
storage hierarchy, you can
see that we put the Optane DC

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:41.420
persistent memory right
in that large gap that

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:44.700
existed between the
storage and the memory.

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:46.190
We also have lots
of improvements

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:50.030
that Intel's been making
in our 3D NAND SSDs.

00:06:50.030 --> 00:06:53.390
And this same media is available
in a solid state drive form

00:06:53.390 --> 00:06:55.050
factor as well.

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:58.100
And so we're working on
really giving the architects

00:06:58.100 --> 00:06:59.810
and software developers
of the future

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:02.480
that full range of
products to design around.

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:06.630
All right.

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:09.245
So when I'm talking to
people about this technology,

00:07:09.245 --> 00:07:10.620
sometimes it takes
a little while

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:13.170
to grasp what it
means when you move

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:15.630
between these different
types and tiers of memory

00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:16.965
and storage.

00:07:16.965 --> 00:07:18.840
So we like to think
about it in terms of what

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:21.070
problem are we trying to solve.

00:07:21.070 --> 00:07:23.670
If you've ever said to yourself,
wow, DRAM is so expensive.

00:07:23.670 --> 00:07:27.210
I wish I could afford to
put more data in memory,

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:33.090
but I just can't, then this has
the ability to help save money

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:35.490
when you pair it with DRAM.

00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:38.970
You also have the ability
to improve your memory

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:40.230
to core ratios.

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:43.030
You can do more with
existing infrastructure.

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:45.570
And then in the app direct
mode that we talked about,

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:47.820
if the solution is
optimized, you also

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:50.190
have the ability to really
help break the performance

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:52.710
barriers that you were seeing
if your application was memory

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:54.120
limited.

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:56.215
And you can see a
lot more performance.

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:00.590
So I have one final slide.

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:02.300
I want to talk to you
about the partners

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:06.380
that we're working with in
the cloud to provide ecosystem

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:08.120
support for this technology.

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:09.050
Yes, I said it.

00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:12.020
Intel is innovating
on the software side.

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:15.020
It's really important that
we-- because we need the ISV

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:17.240
partners to be on board,
we need the service

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:20.210
providers to be on board, and
the OEMs have to be on board.

00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:22.940
You need all of those
elements for the solution.

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:26.150
We've been working over
a decade to make sure

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:28.160
that we have all of the
pieces pulled together

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:29.697
for this solution.

00:08:29.697 --> 00:08:31.280
And these are some
of the key partners

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:34.220
that we've been working with
over the last several years.

00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:36.710
You hear us talk a
lot about SAP Hana.

00:08:36.710 --> 00:08:38.870
But we have a lot
more beyond that.

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:43.880
We're working with SAS, all
of the ISV and OSV providers,

00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:47.700
as well as our partners
here at Google.

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:50.672
So I'm going to bring
up on stage today next--

00:08:50.672 --> 00:08:52.130
and I thank you
guys for your time.

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:53.838
Shideh is going to
talk to you about what

00:08:53.838 --> 00:08:55.357
Google is doing with us.

00:08:55.357 --> 00:08:56.440
SHIDEH SHAHIDI: Thank you.

00:08:56.440 --> 00:08:58.240
Thanks, Kristie.

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:01.520
So I hope you guys can hear me
because I can't hear myself.

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:04.240
I hope I can keep your attention
while the band is playing back

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:04.930
there.

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:07.660
So Kristie talked about
the memory technology

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:09.640
and the landscape in general.

00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:11.980
I am hoping to
let you know about

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:16.360
how this technology is actually
realizing in Google Cloud,

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:18.250
and how you can go
and use it, and what

00:09:18.250 --> 00:09:21.280
are the different use cases that
we've been experimenting with.

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:24.280
So we partnered with
Google around two years ago

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:27.400
on this technology to
bringing it to Google Cloud.

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:30.880
And they announced it
back in March of last--

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:33.610
I'm sorry, the
November of last year.

00:09:33.610 --> 00:09:36.580
That was actually a few months
before our own product line.

00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:39.400
So there went a lot of work from
both sides, engineering work,

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:41.200
into bringing this
product into market.

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:43.660
So they were the first
cloud service provider

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:45.710
to bring this into market.

00:09:45.710 --> 00:09:48.010
If you look at what this
technology translates

00:09:48.010 --> 00:09:51.820
to in terms of the VMs that
you have in Google Cloud,

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:55.480
basically, this is providing
more memory for you.

00:09:55.480 --> 00:09:57.310
If you look at the
VMs that are enabled

00:09:57.310 --> 00:10:01.150
by this Optane memory versus
the previous generation,

00:10:01.150 --> 00:10:05.260
there's actually 4x more memory
per VM that this enables.

00:10:05.260 --> 00:10:08.290
So more memory that is
faster than SSD that's

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:10.930
sitting closer to your compute.

00:10:10.930 --> 00:10:13.540
And it's basically
persistent, right?

00:10:13.540 --> 00:10:16.160
So that's just putting
all of that together,

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:18.280
it creates a lot of
different use cases

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:21.640
that are interesting to enable.

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:24.670
Some of the use cases use the
persistence of the memory.

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:25.330
Some don't.

00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:29.140
So if I went to talk about
some of the use cases

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:31.900
that we've been exploring,
one of the main ones

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:34.540
is basically because you
have this memory that's

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:36.940
per VM that's
close to your core,

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:38.680
you can actually use
this as some sort

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:41.680
of a cache for your I/O
intensive applications, right?

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:45.160
So this would prevent you
from going back and forth

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:47.170
between the IO
and the IO latency

00:10:47.170 --> 00:10:48.708
is going to be prevented.

00:10:48.708 --> 00:10:50.000
So that's one of the use cases.

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:51.583
We've actually done
some experimenting

00:10:51.583 --> 00:10:52.430
with that use case.

00:10:52.430 --> 00:10:56.830
We've done some Spark
SQL analysis inside GCB,

00:10:56.830 --> 00:11:00.460
and we've seen about 4x better
performance for our large data

00:11:00.460 --> 00:11:01.220
sets.

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:04.270
Another use case that
we kind of explored

00:11:04.270 --> 00:11:07.060
was looking at the persistency
of the memory, right?

00:11:07.060 --> 00:11:09.760
So the persistency
of the memory allows

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:13.900
you to basically keep some of
the key values for your data

00:11:13.900 --> 00:11:15.500
inside this memory.

00:11:15.500 --> 00:11:17.860
So when your VM
is restarting, you

00:11:17.860 --> 00:11:20.500
would basically have the
data available close to you

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:23.110
so you wouldn't have to go
through the whole loading

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:23.780
of the data.

00:11:23.780 --> 00:11:26.830
So basically, that translates
to faster restart times.

00:11:26.830 --> 00:11:28.570
We've also looked
into that application

00:11:28.570 --> 00:11:31.360
for different in
memory databases.

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:35.830
And for one of the ISVs that
we've actually worked with,

00:11:35.830 --> 00:11:41.230
we've seen the load times
going from somewhere a little

00:11:41.230 --> 00:11:44.240
more than 20 minutes to
less than 20 seconds.

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:46.850
So that's the kind of
improvements that we've seen.

00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:50.020
And then the last thing
that we've kind of explored

00:11:50.020 --> 00:11:53.470
in this environment is basically
the last value proposition,

00:11:53.470 --> 00:11:57.160
is looking at the memory
size and how you can condense

00:11:57.160 --> 00:11:59.650
more instances into one VM.

00:11:59.650 --> 00:12:01.250
So basically,
different residents.

00:12:01.250 --> 00:12:04.450
So if you have a database
with multiple instances,

00:12:04.450 --> 00:12:07.630
you can compute-- you can
basically put everything

00:12:07.630 --> 00:12:10.450
into one single VM.

00:12:10.450 --> 00:12:14.290
And especially if it's
not very core intensive,

00:12:14.290 --> 00:12:15.790
if it's more memory
intensive, you

00:12:15.790 --> 00:12:17.620
can potentially have
some cost saving there

00:12:17.620 --> 00:12:19.360
because you're not
paying for all the VMs

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:23.200
that you would normally if you
were taking that much memory.

00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:25.260
So those kind of
summarize the value

00:12:25.260 --> 00:12:29.890
propositions inside this
in the card environment.

00:12:29.890 --> 00:12:32.430
Now, I'm going to tell you a
little bit about what actually

00:12:32.430 --> 00:12:35.280
shapes our VMs these
are offered in.

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:38.440
So these memes are both--

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:41.490
if you can see, there are
two different VM shapes

00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:43.770
that this memory is offered in.

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:46.380
The first VM is a
smaller VM shape.

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:49.140
It offers 96 vCPUs.

00:12:49.140 --> 00:12:51.960
It has 624 gigabytes
of DRAM, which

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:57.410
is tied with 3.6 terabytes
of Optane memory.

00:12:57.410 --> 00:13:00.230
There is this bigger
size VM that actually

00:13:00.230 --> 00:13:02.630
has 1.2 terabytes of DRAM.

00:13:02.630 --> 00:13:06.230
It ties back with 5.5
terabytes of Optane memory.

00:13:06.230 --> 00:13:08.030
And the totals of
the memories, you

00:13:08.030 --> 00:13:11.300
can see 3.6 terabytes
and 6.7 terabytes.

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:15.880
Both of these are running on two
socket Intel Xeon processors,

00:13:15.880 --> 00:13:17.540
the next generation, basically.

00:13:17.540 --> 00:13:20.510
This is the Cascade
Lake codename.

00:13:20.510 --> 00:13:22.545
And they're both
single tenant today,

00:13:22.545 --> 00:13:24.920
the way they're offered,
meaning if you get the instance,

00:13:24.920 --> 00:13:28.100
you basically have control
over the whole Optane

00:13:28.100 --> 00:13:31.300
memory and the whole vCPUs.

00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:33.580
The two instances are
both in app direct mode.

00:13:33.580 --> 00:13:36.140
Kristie talked about app
direct mode, I think.

00:13:36.140 --> 00:13:38.520
Andy's going to talk more
about the details of how

00:13:38.520 --> 00:13:40.350
to program for app direct mode.

00:13:40.350 --> 00:13:43.520
But basically, at very high
level, what app direct mode is,

00:13:43.520 --> 00:13:45.720
is it's the mode
where you can take

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:48.220
advantage of the
persistence of the memory.

00:13:48.220 --> 00:13:51.630
So you have the address space
of the Optane separate from

00:13:51.630 --> 00:13:52.980
the address space of the DRAM.

00:13:52.980 --> 00:13:54.960
And you can get--
basically, the application

00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:57.870
can get to choose
where to write what.

00:13:57.870 --> 00:14:00.480
So you can have an
architecture where

00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:04.260
you would store your hot data
into DRAM versus your cold data

00:14:04.260 --> 00:14:06.240
into Apache Pass.

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:07.230
I said it.

00:14:07.230 --> 00:14:09.285
Code word, so Intel Optane.

00:14:11.970 --> 00:14:15.600
So basically, these VMs are
enabled in app direct mode.

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:18.420
And we've been working
with different partners

00:14:18.420 --> 00:14:20.940
to enable their
application for this mode.

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:23.310
And we'll get into some
of the details of what

00:14:23.310 --> 00:14:25.780
we've been doing.

00:14:25.780 --> 00:14:28.840
Just a quick note on how you
can get access to these VMs--

00:14:28.840 --> 00:14:31.510
so that link on top,
this is basically

00:14:31.510 --> 00:14:34.120
where you can request access
for an alpha instance.

00:14:34.120 --> 00:14:36.610
So these offerings are
in alpha right now.

00:14:36.610 --> 00:14:39.670
Google is basically-- this
is the first phase of Google

00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:40.840
products when they launch.

00:14:40.840 --> 00:14:42.280
They go in alpha,
and then there's

00:14:42.280 --> 00:14:44.380
the general availability.

00:14:44.380 --> 00:14:45.910
This is the command
line where you

00:14:45.910 --> 00:14:50.890
can use to basically
instantiate these instances.

00:14:50.890 --> 00:14:52.540
If you notice,
there's a new flag.

00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:54.870
For those of you who are
familiar with the CLI,

00:14:54.870 --> 00:14:58.750
that flag is a new
flag, which basically

00:14:58.750 --> 00:15:00.670
lets you instantiate the VM.

00:15:00.670 --> 00:15:03.560
The local NVDIMM size,
and you can set the size.

00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:06.070
This is set for a 5.5
terabyte instance,

00:15:06.070 --> 00:15:08.260
which basically gives
you the set of 6.7

00:15:08.260 --> 00:15:10.550
terabyte total memory.

00:15:10.550 --> 00:15:13.640
The instances are initially
available in Iowa.

00:15:13.640 --> 00:15:15.950
This is US central.

00:15:15.950 --> 00:15:18.770
But they're going to be
rolling out to other instances.

00:15:18.770 --> 00:15:21.320
So if you want to
experiment with this today,

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:24.570
that's the zone that
you want to pick.

00:15:24.570 --> 00:15:26.990
And then there's always
support from Intel and Google.

00:15:26.990 --> 00:15:29.960
You can go to that website for
different programming models

00:15:29.960 --> 00:15:33.290
if you're bringing your own
application to these instances

00:15:33.290 --> 00:15:36.110
or you're trying with some
of the open source softwares

00:15:36.110 --> 00:15:37.850
that we've been working on.

00:15:37.850 --> 00:15:39.960
So that's the place to
go to get some support.

00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:45.930
So now I want to talk to you
about some of the use cases

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:49.110
that we've been running
on this technology.

00:15:49.110 --> 00:15:52.350
There's a lot of use cases
and different partners

00:15:52.350 --> 00:15:55.710
that we have that have been
talked about during our launch.

00:15:55.710 --> 00:15:59.280
And the collateral is
available for you to go review.

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:01.710
I would actually encourage
you to go and take

00:16:01.710 --> 00:16:04.380
a look if you are thinking
about your own application

00:16:04.380 --> 00:16:07.690
and redoing it in the
Google Cloud environment.

00:16:07.690 --> 00:16:10.350
But here, I just want to
focus on a couple of use cases

00:16:10.350 --> 00:16:13.440
that we have experimented in
the Google Cloud environment

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:16.590
and we've enabled in the
Google Cloud environment.

00:16:16.590 --> 00:16:20.190
We've had access to these
instances for a couple months

00:16:20.190 --> 00:16:22.360
now, so we've been running
different applications.

00:16:22.360 --> 00:16:24.480
One of them has
been a Spark SQL use

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:27.660
case, which our teams have been
running in the Google Cloud

00:16:27.660 --> 00:16:28.645
environment.

00:16:28.645 --> 00:16:30.270
If you look at the
architecture and how

00:16:30.270 --> 00:16:33.810
we've modified the
architecture for our Spark SQL,

00:16:33.810 --> 00:16:36.240
we've actually
created that OEP cache

00:16:36.240 --> 00:16:39.930
that's sitting between your
storage, your Google network

00:16:39.930 --> 00:16:42.120
storage, and your compute.

00:16:42.120 --> 00:16:44.070
And the way that
works is when you

00:16:44.070 --> 00:16:49.110
get a query from your compute
into the IO, first time,

00:16:49.110 --> 00:16:52.830
it goes to the IO, all the
way, all the IO latencies.

00:16:52.830 --> 00:16:58.140
When you get the query, it's
then stored into the OEP cache.

00:16:58.140 --> 00:17:00.270
So for the next iteration,
basically you're

00:17:00.270 --> 00:17:04.000
going to be going right into
the Optane persistent memory.

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:07.140
So you're basically
reverting the year you're not

00:17:07.140 --> 00:17:09.240
seeing the latency of the IO.

00:17:09.240 --> 00:17:11.776
So that's how this is
getting the performance.

00:17:11.776 --> 00:17:13.859
And if you look at the
experiment that we've done,

00:17:13.859 --> 00:17:16.680
the performance is
actually showing around

00:17:16.680 --> 00:17:18.490
4x better performance.

00:17:18.490 --> 00:17:20.250
This is not the final picture.

00:17:20.250 --> 00:17:22.150
We're still working
on some optimizations.

00:17:22.150 --> 00:17:23.700
So we're expecting
to this number

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:26.790
to grow even at two times
what you are seeing here.

00:17:26.790 --> 00:17:28.950
But basically, what I
wanted to emphasize here

00:17:28.950 --> 00:17:34.290
is that with that package, with
the OEP package in Spark SQL,

00:17:34.290 --> 00:17:37.650
you can basically get up to
8x performance improvement

00:17:37.650 --> 00:17:38.495
from your queries.

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:43.470
What I wanted to highlight,
this is for a case

00:17:43.470 --> 00:17:46.410
where your data is really big.

00:17:46.410 --> 00:17:48.270
It's a big data set.

00:17:48.270 --> 00:17:50.040
It doesn't fit in your DRAM.

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:51.120
It fits in the cache.

00:17:51.120 --> 00:17:54.300
I mean, if you can implement
that same OEP on a DRAM,

00:17:54.300 --> 00:17:55.640
the data wouldn't fit on it.

00:17:55.640 --> 00:17:58.890
It would fit on
the Optane memory.

00:17:58.890 --> 00:18:01.830
On the other end of
the spectrum of cases

00:18:01.830 --> 00:18:05.280
for Spark is a data
set that is small,

00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:07.560
that doesn't really
require as much memory.

00:18:07.560 --> 00:18:09.840
For those cases, you
still have the advantage

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:13.980
of the cost savings that you do
because your per gigabyte price

00:18:13.980 --> 00:18:19.260
of Optane versus DRAM is lower
in Google Cloud environment.

00:18:19.260 --> 00:18:21.180
So across the
spectrum, you can see

00:18:21.180 --> 00:18:24.400
there are different value
propositions for the memory.

00:18:24.400 --> 00:18:25.800
This is one side
of the spectrum,

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:29.480
and the other side
is the cost saving.

00:18:29.480 --> 00:18:31.460
And then one more
note, so this package,

00:18:31.460 --> 00:18:33.990
the OAP package is
now open source.

00:18:33.990 --> 00:18:36.140
It's available on GitHub.

00:18:36.140 --> 00:18:38.000
The address is down
there on this slide.

00:18:38.000 --> 00:18:42.110
If you want to download it and
experiment with it, feel free.

00:18:42.110 --> 00:18:43.130
So it's there.

00:18:46.778 --> 00:18:49.650
And then another use case
that we've been looking at

00:18:49.650 --> 00:18:50.820
is a similar use case.

00:18:50.820 --> 00:18:52.860
It's with Hadoop HBase.

00:18:52.860 --> 00:18:53.650
Same thing.

00:18:53.650 --> 00:18:55.920
So we have introduced
this bucket cache

00:18:55.920 --> 00:19:00.360
between the storage
and the compute.

00:19:00.360 --> 00:19:03.900
When you get a client
request from HBase client,

00:19:03.900 --> 00:19:05.250
it basically goes--

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:07.650
if you can follow
that number one,

00:19:07.650 --> 00:19:11.130
that's the flow of
your client requests.

00:19:11.130 --> 00:19:14.370
It's going into the
network storage.

00:19:14.370 --> 00:19:15.840
And then the next
time around, when

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:18.242
you make a request
from data, it's

00:19:18.242 --> 00:19:19.950
actually going to be
in the bucket cache.

00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:21.870
So very similar to
the Spark use case,

00:19:21.870 --> 00:19:25.230
it's just for a
different application.

00:19:25.230 --> 00:19:26.730
And then if you
look at that, that's

00:19:26.730 --> 00:19:29.910
actually around 33x
better performance.

00:19:29.910 --> 00:19:32.250
Again, this is the case
where your data does not

00:19:32.250 --> 00:19:35.040
fit in a bucket cache
that's won on a DRAM,

00:19:35.040 --> 00:19:38.550
versus when your data actually
fits into Optane memory.

00:19:38.550 --> 00:19:40.830
So 33x for a better
performance, and then

00:19:40.830 --> 00:19:42.430
on the other end
of the spectrum,

00:19:42.430 --> 00:19:46.482
you still have the cost
saving benefit for the memory.

00:19:46.482 --> 00:19:48.840
So again, this is going
to be open source.

00:19:48.840 --> 00:19:53.670
I think that's the--
you can go to that link

00:19:53.670 --> 00:19:58.080
at the bottom of the
slide and get the package.

00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:02.830
So basically, these are some
of the-- some of the use cases,

00:20:02.830 --> 00:20:06.150
like I said, there are multiple
many different other use cases

00:20:06.150 --> 00:20:07.350
that we've been working on.

00:20:07.350 --> 00:20:08.760
We're going to be
bringing those,

00:20:08.760 --> 00:20:11.500
enabling those in the
Google Cloud environment.

00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:13.170
We're also working
with different ISVs.

00:20:13.170 --> 00:20:14.640
You saw that on the slide there.

00:20:14.640 --> 00:20:17.280
ISVs who are taking
our optimizations

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:19.372
and putting them inside
their application.

00:20:19.372 --> 00:20:21.330
And they're going to be
available through those

00:20:21.330 --> 00:20:24.720
as we roll this
product out and as we

00:20:24.720 --> 00:20:28.370
roll the partnerships out.

00:20:28.370 --> 00:20:29.950
I think that's it for me.

00:20:29.950 --> 00:20:33.792
Now I invite Andy to talk more
about the programming model.

00:20:33.792 --> 00:20:34.750
ANDY RUDOFF: Thank you.

00:20:37.415 --> 00:20:38.290
All right, thank you.

00:20:38.290 --> 00:20:42.400
So we heard from Kristie that
there's this new Optane DC

00:20:42.400 --> 00:20:43.620
persistent memory.

00:20:43.620 --> 00:20:46.450
We heard from Shideh that
it's available on GCP.

00:20:46.450 --> 00:20:50.050
And so I'm here to talk about if
it's on GCP, how do you use it?

00:20:50.050 --> 00:20:52.340
Especially if
you're a developer,

00:20:52.340 --> 00:20:54.430
you want to develop,
say, a nice cloud

00:20:54.430 --> 00:20:58.660
application that takes advantage
of Intel Optane DC persisten

00:20:58.660 --> 00:20:59.910
memory.

00:20:59.910 --> 00:21:02.643
So I'm a software engineer.

00:21:02.643 --> 00:21:04.810
And I can prove it because
they told me to dress up,

00:21:04.810 --> 00:21:06.310
and this is what I wore.

00:21:06.310 --> 00:21:10.128
So I'm really going to
talk to you about software.

00:21:10.128 --> 00:21:12.670
The first thing that a software
engineer would have to decide

00:21:12.670 --> 00:21:14.560
is, well, how do I
want to use this stuff?

00:21:14.560 --> 00:21:17.470
And really, there's kind of
two broad categories here.

00:21:17.470 --> 00:21:19.070
Do you want to use
it as volatile?

00:21:19.070 --> 00:21:21.297
It is persistent
memory, but maybe you

00:21:21.297 --> 00:21:22.630
don't care that it's persistent.

00:21:22.630 --> 00:21:24.830
You just want to use
it for its capacity.

00:21:24.830 --> 00:21:26.915
And so on the left side,
I've sort of showed you.

00:21:26.915 --> 00:21:27.790
You have this choice.

00:21:27.790 --> 00:21:30.790
If you decide just to use
this for its capacity,

00:21:30.790 --> 00:21:34.100
you can do the lowest
impact to your application.

00:21:34.100 --> 00:21:37.090
You can use memory mode,
which Kristie explained,

00:21:37.090 --> 00:21:40.565
just makes the system memory
look like it's huge, right?

00:21:40.565 --> 00:21:41.940
Your application
is not modified.

00:21:41.940 --> 00:21:43.315
In fact, the OS
doesn't even need

00:21:43.315 --> 00:21:44.830
modifications for memory mode.

00:21:44.830 --> 00:21:47.180
It's completely
transparent to software.

00:21:47.180 --> 00:21:49.160
OK, so that's one way to use it.

00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:54.760
But if you want to do a little
better job on where data gets

00:21:54.760 --> 00:21:56.530
placed, instead of
hardware deciding

00:21:56.530 --> 00:21:59.830
what gets placed into DRAM and
what gets placed into Optane

00:21:59.830 --> 00:22:02.830
memory, if you want an
application to decide

00:22:02.830 --> 00:22:06.880
what gets placed into DRAM and
what gets placed on the Optane

00:22:06.880 --> 00:22:10.022
media, you could use
the app direct mode.

00:22:10.022 --> 00:22:11.980
But still, you don't care
on the left hand side

00:22:11.980 --> 00:22:13.540
here that it's persistent.

00:22:13.540 --> 00:22:16.300
It's called a volatile
app direct usage.

00:22:16.300 --> 00:22:19.510
And actually, it's a growing
number of our use cases

00:22:19.510 --> 00:22:20.710
use this method.

00:22:20.710 --> 00:22:23.530
It's pretty easy
type of programming.

00:22:23.530 --> 00:22:25.870
You just decide when
you allocate memory,

00:22:25.870 --> 00:22:27.490
do I want to just
allocate from DRAM,

00:22:27.490 --> 00:22:29.830
or do I want to allocate
from this other tier

00:22:29.830 --> 00:22:31.990
that happens to be a
little slower than DRAM,

00:22:31.990 --> 00:22:33.370
but it's a lot cheaper, right?

00:22:33.370 --> 00:22:35.740
So you put a lot of
big data structures

00:22:35.740 --> 00:22:38.830
into the Optane
media, and then you

00:22:38.830 --> 00:22:43.990
put the really latency sensitive
hot data structures into DRAM.

00:22:43.990 --> 00:22:47.320
On the right hand side
is the set of decisions

00:22:47.320 --> 00:22:50.110
you make if you decide you
do care about persistence.

00:22:50.110 --> 00:22:54.010
Maybe you want to use this
to store some in memory data

00:22:54.010 --> 00:22:56.740
structure that is persistent.

00:22:56.740 --> 00:22:59.950
It's there even if the
machine gets rebooted

00:22:59.950 --> 00:23:02.020
or even if it loses power.

00:23:02.020 --> 00:23:04.443
So again, you have kind
of two choices to make.

00:23:04.443 --> 00:23:06.610
You can say, well, OK, I
want to use the persistence

00:23:06.610 --> 00:23:08.620
but I don't want to
modify my application.

00:23:08.620 --> 00:23:11.560
For that, we just have
the normal storage APIs.

00:23:11.560 --> 00:23:14.230
It looks like a very
fast SSD, right?

00:23:14.230 --> 00:23:15.520
The world's fastest SSD.

00:23:15.520 --> 00:23:20.290
You just use the normal file
APIs that you're used to using.

00:23:20.290 --> 00:23:21.070
They work.

00:23:21.070 --> 00:23:23.700
They just happen to be
mind numbingly fast.

00:23:23.700 --> 00:23:26.200
On the other hand, if you want
to squeeze every bit of value

00:23:26.200 --> 00:23:28.630
that you can out of
the Optane media,

00:23:28.630 --> 00:23:30.050
then you modify
your application.

00:23:30.050 --> 00:23:32.008
And this is what I'm
showing on the right here.

00:23:32.008 --> 00:23:34.120
You modify your
application to go ahead

00:23:34.120 --> 00:23:38.650
and map this persistent memory
right into the application.

00:23:38.650 --> 00:23:40.540
And then you get to
use loads and stores,

00:23:40.540 --> 00:23:42.310
just like you do with DRAM.

00:23:42.310 --> 00:23:46.182
So this thing on the right
here, it's the highest lift.

00:23:46.182 --> 00:23:48.640
It's the most change that you
might make to an application,

00:23:48.640 --> 00:23:51.160
but it's also the most value
that you can squeeze out

00:23:51.160 --> 00:23:54.230
of the Optane media.

00:23:54.230 --> 00:23:58.445
So when I started this
project a long time ago,

00:23:58.445 --> 00:24:00.070
I knew that we would
need a programming

00:24:00.070 --> 00:24:04.120
model to expose all the things
that I just told you about.

00:24:04.120 --> 00:24:08.200
We formed a working
group in SNIA,

00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:11.170
which is the Storage Networking
Industry Association.

00:24:11.170 --> 00:24:13.580
It's essentially
a standards body.

00:24:13.580 --> 00:24:15.965
And we got a bunch of
companies to come and join us

00:24:15.965 --> 00:24:16.840
in the working group.

00:24:16.840 --> 00:24:18.860
That was about
five years ago now,

00:24:18.860 --> 00:24:21.800
and there's over 50 companies
in this working group

00:24:21.800 --> 00:24:25.360
now, a lot of them that you
would recognize as operating

00:24:25.360 --> 00:24:27.880
systems vendors and as ISVs.

00:24:27.880 --> 00:24:30.220
And what we came up
with is this model.

00:24:30.220 --> 00:24:32.410
And I've updated the
model a little bit

00:24:32.410 --> 00:24:34.718
to use the term
app direct, which

00:24:34.718 --> 00:24:36.760
is what Intel calls the
persistent memory that we

00:24:36.760 --> 00:24:38.480
used in this model.

00:24:38.480 --> 00:24:41.810
And you can see at
the bottom here,

00:24:41.810 --> 00:24:44.645
I'm showing all the persistent
memory installed in the system.

00:24:44.645 --> 00:24:46.270
Some of it might be
in that memory mode

00:24:46.270 --> 00:24:47.353
that I mentioned before.

00:24:47.353 --> 00:24:49.103
That's transparent to
software, so I don't

00:24:49.103 --> 00:24:50.620
have to talk about it anymore.

00:24:50.620 --> 00:24:52.360
The rest of it is
persistent, and you

00:24:52.360 --> 00:24:55.630
can see we've added drivers
to the operating systems.

00:24:55.630 --> 00:24:58.060
This is all true today
in Linux, and Windows,

00:24:58.060 --> 00:24:59.390
and other operating systems.

00:24:59.390 --> 00:25:01.390
I'll give you a
list in a minute.

00:25:01.390 --> 00:25:04.600
And that driver exposes things
through this middle path

00:25:04.600 --> 00:25:05.680
here as storage.

00:25:05.680 --> 00:25:07.180
This is what I was telling you.

00:25:07.180 --> 00:25:09.070
Applications can use
this programming model

00:25:09.070 --> 00:25:10.390
without being modified.

00:25:10.390 --> 00:25:13.142
They just think they're
talking to a really fast SSD.

00:25:13.142 --> 00:25:14.350
But that's not the cool part.

00:25:14.350 --> 00:25:16.308
The cool part is what
happens on the right hand

00:25:16.308 --> 00:25:17.410
side of this diagram.

00:25:17.410 --> 00:25:19.150
On the right hand
side of this diagram,

00:25:19.150 --> 00:25:22.410
an application opens up a file
on a persistent memory of where

00:25:22.410 --> 00:25:24.970
a file system and maps it in.

00:25:24.970 --> 00:25:26.800
And you get this
far right blue arrow

00:25:26.800 --> 00:25:29.800
that I'm showing here,
where an application has

00:25:29.800 --> 00:25:32.170
mapped the persistent
memory directly

00:25:32.170 --> 00:25:34.010
into its address space.

00:25:34.010 --> 00:25:35.650
This means-- just
think about this.

00:25:35.650 --> 00:25:38.310
An application does
a load instruction,

00:25:38.310 --> 00:25:40.320
and it gets a value
off persistence

00:25:40.320 --> 00:25:42.580
without any kernel code running.

00:25:42.580 --> 00:25:43.080
Right?

00:25:43.080 --> 00:25:44.580
There's no context switching.

00:25:44.580 --> 00:25:45.420
There's no IO.

00:25:45.420 --> 00:25:48.960
There's no interrupts,
no kernel code runs.

00:25:48.960 --> 00:25:51.030
One instruction means
that you're fetching

00:25:51.030 --> 00:25:52.950
from persistence, right?

00:25:52.950 --> 00:25:55.040
That's pretty cool.

00:25:58.200 --> 00:26:00.900
So, that all sounds
great, but it

00:26:00.900 --> 00:26:02.200
does come with some challenges.

00:26:02.200 --> 00:26:04.033
I don't want to make
it sound like it's just

00:26:04.033 --> 00:26:05.500
the easiest thing in the world.

00:26:05.500 --> 00:26:09.570
For example, if you put a data
structure in persistent memory,

00:26:09.570 --> 00:26:11.760
you want it to be
something you can

00:26:11.760 --> 00:26:15.060
get to after the machine crashes
if you have a crash or a power

00:26:15.060 --> 00:26:16.060
failure.

00:26:16.060 --> 00:26:17.920
And so it needs
to be consistent.

00:26:17.920 --> 00:26:19.980
Now file systems do this
all the time, right?

00:26:19.980 --> 00:26:21.850
If you're making
changes to a file system

00:26:21.850 --> 00:26:23.480
and you lose power,
when you come back,

00:26:23.480 --> 00:26:25.230
the file system has a
journal or something

00:26:25.230 --> 00:26:26.760
that makes it consistent.

00:26:26.760 --> 00:26:30.120
But programmers are not used to
doing this with data structures

00:26:30.120 --> 00:26:31.930
that they just create in memory.

00:26:31.930 --> 00:26:34.800
So that's kind of a new kind
of programming, consistency

00:26:34.800 --> 00:26:36.010
with the face of failure.

00:26:36.010 --> 00:26:39.420
What you really want are some
sort of ways of transactionally

00:26:39.420 --> 00:26:41.730
updating your data, right?

00:26:41.730 --> 00:26:44.200
And how about keeping track
of which persistent memory

00:26:44.200 --> 00:26:46.620
you've allocated and which
you haven't yet allocated?

00:26:46.620 --> 00:26:50.040
Again, that's a little trickier
than it is with normal DRAM

00:26:50.040 --> 00:26:52.658
because it has to be
a persistent heap.

00:26:52.658 --> 00:26:54.450
And so what you want
is a persistent memory

00:26:54.450 --> 00:26:57.750
aware allocator to
help you with that.

00:26:57.750 --> 00:27:01.560
Also, there are some algorithms
that are different in DRAM

00:27:01.560 --> 00:27:04.290
than they are with
persistent memory.

00:27:04.290 --> 00:27:05.940
What's the fastest
way of copying bytes

00:27:05.940 --> 00:27:07.140
to persistent memory?

00:27:07.140 --> 00:27:10.740
Well, we have different
instructions in Intel

00:27:10.740 --> 00:27:12.750
that we've gone through,
and benchmarked,

00:27:12.750 --> 00:27:14.550
and done measurements
on to figure out

00:27:14.550 --> 00:27:16.842
what's the best way of doing
a lot of these operations.

00:27:16.842 --> 00:27:18.480
So you want these
tuned libraries,

00:27:18.480 --> 00:27:20.688
so that you don't have to
reinvent this all the time.

00:27:20.688 --> 00:27:22.397
And then finally, when
you're programming

00:27:22.397 --> 00:27:24.400
in your favorite
language-- for example,

00:27:24.400 --> 00:27:28.140
you're writing a web based
application in JavaScript,

00:27:28.140 --> 00:27:30.390
you want something
that gives you access

00:27:30.390 --> 00:27:32.520
to persistent memory in
a way that makes sense

00:27:32.520 --> 00:27:34.090
to a JavaScript programmer.

00:27:34.090 --> 00:27:34.590
Right?

00:27:34.590 --> 00:27:38.850
You don't want to be going
through some weird API

00:27:38.850 --> 00:27:41.490
to call some C routine that
doesn't sound like JavaScript

00:27:41.490 --> 00:27:42.330
at all, right?

00:27:42.330 --> 00:27:43.500
You want it to be idiomatic.

00:27:43.500 --> 00:27:44.583
You want it to make sense.

00:27:44.583 --> 00:27:46.620
If you're writing in C,
it should look like C.

00:27:46.620 --> 00:27:48.983
If you're writing C++,
it should look like C++.

00:27:48.983 --> 00:27:51.150
If you're writing in Java,
it should look like Java.

00:27:51.150 --> 00:27:52.860
So we want language bindings.

00:27:52.860 --> 00:27:54.810
So y'all know what
I'm leading up to.

00:27:54.810 --> 00:27:57.660
We took all of these things,
all of these requirements,

00:27:57.660 --> 00:27:59.580
and we decided
that we could build

00:27:59.580 --> 00:28:02.197
a attuned vendor neutral--

00:28:02.197 --> 00:28:04.530
not just for this product,
but for any persistent memory

00:28:04.530 --> 00:28:05.640
product--

00:28:05.640 --> 00:28:08.040
vendor [INAUDIBLE]
set of libraries

00:28:08.040 --> 00:28:11.330
to make persistent memory
programming easier.

00:28:11.330 --> 00:28:13.830
Those libraries are called the
Persistent Memory Development

00:28:13.830 --> 00:28:15.610
Kit or PMDK.

00:28:15.610 --> 00:28:18.570
There's the URL
up there, pmem.io.

00:28:18.570 --> 00:28:21.600
And the slide has a lot
of information on it.

00:28:21.600 --> 00:28:24.090
But all of this
information is on pmem.io.

00:28:24.090 --> 00:28:25.470
You can go find it.

00:28:25.470 --> 00:28:28.350
You can see where the libraries
fit into that architecture

00:28:28.350 --> 00:28:30.210
that I said before on the right.

00:28:30.210 --> 00:28:32.940
See where the application is
now getting direct load store

00:28:32.940 --> 00:28:34.290
access?

00:28:34.290 --> 00:28:36.060
I'm not adding another
software layer.

00:28:36.060 --> 00:28:39.540
I'm pulling in algorithms,
just what you need, right?

00:28:39.540 --> 00:28:40.820
We call it shrink to fit.

00:28:40.820 --> 00:28:43.380
Pull in just the amount
of software that you need.

00:28:43.380 --> 00:28:46.320
You need transactions, pull in
the library for transactions.

00:28:46.320 --> 00:28:51.090
You need something to look like
a JavaScript key value store,

00:28:51.090 --> 00:28:52.980
just pull in that library.

00:28:52.980 --> 00:28:55.642
And so I've shown
you on the slide what

00:28:55.642 --> 00:28:56.850
several of the libraries are.

00:28:56.850 --> 00:28:58.267
There are some low
level ones that

00:28:58.267 --> 00:28:59.880
hide instructions from you.

00:28:59.880 --> 00:29:02.197
They abstract away some
of those hardware details.

00:29:02.197 --> 00:29:03.780
There are some
libraries in the middle

00:29:03.780 --> 00:29:05.310
that handle transactions.

00:29:05.310 --> 00:29:07.060
That's pretty
tricky programming.

00:29:07.060 --> 00:29:08.570
So we get a lot of
use out of that.

00:29:08.570 --> 00:29:10.320
And then we've built
on top of these a lot

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:12.240
of language bindings,
like I'm showing you.

00:29:12.240 --> 00:29:15.720
C, C++, there's some
experimental Python bindings.

00:29:15.720 --> 00:29:18.120
There's some Java bindings
we've been working on.

00:29:18.120 --> 00:29:21.420
And like I said, there's even
some JavaScript bindings, which

00:29:21.420 --> 00:29:23.980
are so new, they didn't
make it on this slide.

00:29:23.980 --> 00:29:26.970
So this gives you a general idea
of what the Persistent Memory

00:29:26.970 --> 00:29:28.110
Development Kit is.

00:29:31.670 --> 00:29:35.640
In the ecosystem, like I say,
we created this SNIA working

00:29:35.640 --> 00:29:36.140
group.

00:29:36.140 --> 00:29:38.590
We had a lot of the operating
system vendors there.

00:29:38.590 --> 00:29:40.340
So everything that
I've been talking about

00:29:40.340 --> 00:29:43.010
is available upstream
in Linux already.

00:29:43.010 --> 00:29:44.690
I'm showing you the
Linux kernel version

00:29:44.690 --> 00:29:46.566
that we recommend
up there, 4.19.

00:29:46.566 --> 00:29:49.340
There are two file
systems in Linux

00:29:49.340 --> 00:29:50.960
that are persistent
memory aware--

00:29:50.960 --> 00:29:54.740
ext4 and XFS are both already
persistent memory aware.

00:29:54.740 --> 00:29:57.787
In Windows Server 2019,
all these changes went in.

00:29:57.787 --> 00:29:59.870
And the file system that's
persistent memory aware

00:29:59.870 --> 00:30:01.670
there is NTFS.

00:30:01.670 --> 00:30:04.820
VMware, vSphere 6.7
and later also supports

00:30:04.820 --> 00:30:05.570
persistent memory.

00:30:05.570 --> 00:30:10.500
So everything that I just said
is true in a VMware guest,

00:30:10.500 --> 00:30:11.000
right?

00:30:11.000 --> 00:30:13.250
Including a program--
and just think about what

00:30:13.250 --> 00:30:14.810
this takes to make it work.

00:30:14.810 --> 00:30:19.490
A program running in a
virtualized guest does a load,

00:30:19.490 --> 00:30:21.650
and it thinks it's talking
to persistent memory.

00:30:21.650 --> 00:30:24.500
And it is because VMware
actually put all the plumbing

00:30:24.500 --> 00:30:26.750
in so that it's actually
talking directly

00:30:26.750 --> 00:30:28.460
to the hardware
inside the guest.

00:30:28.460 --> 00:30:30.240
That's pretty cool.

00:30:30.240 --> 00:30:32.390
There's the common
distros RHEL and SLES.

00:30:32.390 --> 00:30:35.120
I put up there Ubuntu.

00:30:35.120 --> 00:30:39.130
Java has some pretty exciting
changes coming in the upcoming

00:30:39.130 --> 00:30:40.870
JDK release and so on.

00:30:40.870 --> 00:30:42.290
And what else I
put on this slide

00:30:42.290 --> 00:30:44.185
is I just thought
I'd show you what

00:30:44.185 --> 00:30:46.560
does it look like when you
put all these pieces together?

00:30:46.560 --> 00:30:49.760
So we have a proof of concept
that we did with Cassandra.

00:30:49.760 --> 00:30:51.290
And you can see it here.

00:30:51.290 --> 00:30:54.410
At the bottom, it starts with
exposing the persistent memory

00:30:54.410 --> 00:30:56.720
using the persistent
memory aware file system

00:30:56.720 --> 00:30:59.370
that I mentioned before.

00:30:59.370 --> 00:31:02.630
You can see we used PMDK
here to first abstract away

00:31:02.630 --> 00:31:07.280
the hardware details and then
provide some transactions.

00:31:07.280 --> 00:31:09.920
On top of that, we
have the Java bindings.

00:31:09.920 --> 00:31:13.520
The low level persistence
library for Java is there.

00:31:13.520 --> 00:31:15.020
And then we made a
persistent memory

00:31:15.020 --> 00:31:17.430
aware version of Cassandra.

00:31:17.430 --> 00:31:21.080
So what's interesting about this
picture is the app at the top

00:31:21.080 --> 00:31:22.937
has not been modified.

00:31:22.937 --> 00:31:24.770
The app is just talking
to Cassandra the way

00:31:24.770 --> 00:31:26.130
it always has.

00:31:26.130 --> 00:31:29.490
But Cassandra and below knows
about persistent memory.

00:31:29.490 --> 00:31:32.480
And this is really
a common pattern

00:31:32.480 --> 00:31:34.730
that we've been seeing
when we modify applications

00:31:34.730 --> 00:31:36.063
for persistent memory.

00:31:36.063 --> 00:31:38.480
The knowledge that there's
persistent memory in the system

00:31:38.480 --> 00:31:41.138
only goes so far up the
stack, and then at some point,

00:31:41.138 --> 00:31:42.430
everything above is unmodified.

00:31:42.430 --> 00:31:45.600
It just thinks it's
operating as usual.

00:31:45.600 --> 00:31:46.820
So it kind of shows you.

00:31:46.820 --> 00:31:49.080
So did I modify the app?

00:31:49.080 --> 00:31:52.250
Well, some people would say no
because the app in my picture

00:31:52.250 --> 00:31:53.340
hasn't changed.

00:31:53.340 --> 00:31:55.590
Some people will say yes
because the app is Cassandra.

00:31:55.590 --> 00:31:56.680
So decide for yourself.

00:31:56.680 --> 00:31:59.180
But you can see the knowledge
of persistent memory only goes

00:31:59.180 --> 00:32:00.770
so far up the stack.

00:32:00.770 --> 00:32:05.320
[MUSIC PLAYING]

